                                        <li><strong>Social Democrats:</strong> main party in setting it up.</li>
                                        <li><strong>Centre Party:</strong> protect Catholic Church.</li>
                                        <li><strong>Democratic & People's Parties:</strong> middle class support.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <!-- Rebellions placed BELOW the line -->
                        <div class="border-t border-beige-200 pt-3 space-y-3">
                            <h4 class="font-bold text-brown-800 mb-2">Early Rebellions & Uprisings (1919–1923)</h4>
                            
                            <!-- 1. Spartacist Uprising -->
                            <div class="bg-beige-100 p-3 rounded-xl border border-beige-200 space-y-1.5 text-xs">
                                <strong class="text-brown-900 font-bold block">1. The Spartacist Uprising (January 1919)</strong>
                                <p class="text-brown-800"><strong>Who they were:</strong> A radical communist group led by Rosa Luxemburg and Karl Liebknecht. Mistrusted the new democratic government’s ability to help the working class.</p>
                                <p class="text-brown-800"><strong>Goal:</strong> To spark a full-scale communist revolution in Germany, modeled after the 1917 Russian Revolution. Took over key newspaper headquarters in Berlin, expecting widespread public support that never materialized.</p>
                                <p class="text-brown-800"><strong>Suppression & Outcome:</strong> The government used the Freikorps and the regular army to crush the revolt. Over 100 workers were killed. Badly planned with no backup; Luxemburg and Liebknecht were captured and murdered, crippling the movement.</p>
                            </div>

                            <!-- 2. Kapp Putsch -->
                            <div class="bg-beige-100 p-3 rounded-xl border border-beige-200 space-y-1.5 text-xs">
                                <strong class="text-brown-900 font-bold block">2. The Kapp Putsch (March 1920)</strong>
                                <p class="text-brown-800"><strong>Who they were:</strong> Led by Wolfgang Kapp, a nationalist/monarchist, alongside disgruntled right-wing Freikorps units. Triggered when the Weimar Republic attempted to downsize the army and disband the Freikorps.</p>
                                <p class="text-brown-800"><strong>The Uprising:</strong> Refused to disband; 12,000 armed Freikorps troops marched on Berlin to overthrow the democratic government and install a right-wing regime. The regular army refused to fire on fellow right-wing soldiers ("Reichswehr does not fire on Reichswehr"), forcing the government to flee to Stuttgart.</p>
                                <p class="text-brown-800"><strong>Outcome:</strong> Defeated when the government called a general strike in Berlin. Services stopped completely, making it impossible for Kapp to govern; after four days Kapp fled and the Weimar government returned.</p>
                            </div>

                            <!-- 3. Munich Putsch -->
                            <div class="bg-beige-100 p-3 rounded-xl border border-beige-200 space-y-1.5 text-xs">
                                <strong class="text-brown-900 font-bold block">3. The Munich Putsch (November 1923)</strong>
                                <p class="text-brown-800"><strong>Goal:</strong> To overthrow Germany's "weak" democratic system and establish General Ludendorff as leader.</p>
                                <p class="text-brown-800"><strong>Why they thought it would succeed:</strong> Nazi Party had grown to 55,000 members by 1923, Bavarian regional support, SA acting as private army, and presumed support from Kahr and Ludendorff.</p>
                                <p class="text-brown-800"><strong>Reasons for Failure:</strong> Bad planning, mistakes of Hitler and Ludendorff, Kahr and Lossow withdrawing support at the critical moment, and swift action by the Weimar government and police.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- TOPIC 3: Hyperinflation (Dedicated Full-Width Square) -->
                <div x-show="selectedTopicFilter === 'all' || selectedTopicFilter === 'hyperinflation'" class="bg-beige-50 rounded-2xl border border-beige-300 shadow-sm overflow-hidden flex flex-col md:col-span-2">
