                    </div>
                    <div class="p-6 space-y-4 flex-grow text-sm">
                        <!-- Hyperinflation Chain Diagram (Left to Right) -->
                        <div class="bg-beige-100 p-4 rounded-2xl border border-beige-300 space-y-3">
                            <h4 class="font-bold text-brown-900 flex items-center space-x-2 text-xs">
                                <i class="fa-solid fa-diagram-next text-accent-burgundy"></i>
                                <span>The 4-Step Path to Hyperinflation (Left to Right)</span>
                            </h4>
                            
                            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-4 gap-2 text-xs items-center">
                                <div class="bg-beige-50 p-3 rounded-xl border border-beige-300 shadow-sm h-full flex flex-col justify-between relative">
                                    <div>
                                        <span class="font-bold text-accent-burgundy block mb-1">1. Treaty of Versailles (1919)</span>
                                        <p class="text-brown-800">$6.6bn in war reparations weakened the post-war German economy. The German government could not pay the reparation payments.</p>
                                    </div>
                                    <div class="hidden md:block absolute -right-3 top-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 text-brown-600 z-10 bg-beige-200 rounded-full w-6 h-6 text-center leading-6 shadow-sm"><i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right text-[10px]"></i></div>
                                </div>

                                <div class="bg-beige-50 p-3 rounded-xl border border-beige-300 shadow-sm h-full flex flex-col justify-between relative">
                                    <div>
                                        <span class="font-bold text-accent-burgundy block mb-1">2. Ruhr Occupation (Jan 1923)</span>
                                        <p class="text-brown-800">In response to the Germans not paying back reparations the France & Belgium occupy Ruhr valley because it was the industrial centre of Germany and was producing a lot of money. The government encouraged German workers to launch passive resistance strikes.</p>
                                    </div>
                                    <div class="hidden md:block absolute -right-3 top-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 text-brown-600 z-10 bg-beige-200 rounded-full w-6 h-6 text-center leading-6 shadow-sm"><i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right text-[10px]"></i></div>
                                </div>

                                <div class="bg-beige-50 p-3 rounded-xl border border-beige-300 shadow-sm h-full flex flex-col justify-between relative">
                                    <div>
                                        <span class="font-bold text-accent-burgundy block mb-1">3. Excessive Money Printing</span>
                                        <p class="text-brown-800">The government needed to pay the Ruhr workers who were on strike but had no money to do so as they weren't producing anything. The government then prints massive amounts of paper money to pay striking workers and state debts.</p>
                                    </div>
                                    <div class="hidden md:block absolute -right-3 top-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 text-brown-600 z-10 bg-beige-200 rounded-full w-6 h-6 text-center leading-6 shadow-sm"><i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right text-[10px]"></i></div>
                                </div>

                                <div class="bg-beige-50 p-3 rounded-xl border border-beige-300 shadow-sm h-full flex flex-col justify-between">
                                    <div>
                                        <span class="font-bold text-accent-burgundy block mb-1">4. Hyperinflation (1923)</span>
                                        <p class="text-brown-800">Currency loses all value; prices skyrocket daily & hourly (1 USD = 2bn marks). People turned to cigarettes as currency instead, stole wheel barrows rather than the cash inside it, burned money to keep warm.</p>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-xs">
                            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 p-3 rounded-xl space-y-1">
                                <strong class="text-red-900 font-bold block mb-1">Impacts - Losers:</strong>
                                <ul class="list-disc pl-4 space-y-1 text-red-900">
                                    <li><strong>Workers:</strong> wages didn't keep up with price rises / food shortages because farmers wouldn't sell for worthless money – led to starvation deaths.</li>
                                    <li><strong>Older people:</strong> pensions worth less and they had no pay rises.</li>
                                    <li><strong>Savers:</strong> savings that could have bought a house now barely bought a loaf of bread.</li>
                                </ul>
                            </div>
                            <div class="bg-green-50 border border-green-200 p-3 rounded-xl space-y-1">
                                <strong class="text-green-900 font-bold block mb-1">Impacts - Winners:</strong>
                                <ul class="list-disc pl-4 space-y-1 text-red-900">
                                    <li><strong>Upper classes:</strong> invested their money so not as badly affected.</li>
                                    <li><strong>Debtors:</strong> loans worth less so easier to pay off.</li>
                                    <li><strong>Business people:</strong> could pay back business loans, prices increased more than wages leading to profits.</li>
                                </ul>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
